### SEC-002: Mass Assignment via Unfiltered User Input in Database Operations

**Severity:** Critical
**Confidence:** 98%
**CWE:** CWE-915 (Improperly Controlled Modification of Dynamically-Determined Object Attributes)

**Evidence:**

- **Line 3:** `req.body` is spread directly into the SQL UPDATE query with no field filtering:
  ```typescript
  await db.query("UPDATE users SET ? WHERE id = ?", [req.body, req.params.id]);
  ```
- **Line 8:** `req.body` is spread directly into a new object and passed to the SQL INSERT query with no field filtering:
  ```typescript
  const user = { ...req.body, createdAt: new Date() };
  await db.query("INSERT INTO users SET ?", [user]);
  ```

**Concrete Exploit Path:**

An attacker sending `PUT /api/users/123` with body `{ "name": "Alice", "isAdmin": true, "role": "superadmin" }` would have those fields written directly to the `users` table, achieving privilege escalation. Similarly, `POST /api/register` with `{ "email": "a@b.com", "password": "x", "role": "admin" }` creates an administrative account.

The comment on line 2 (`// Directly spreading user input into DB update — allows setting isAdmin, role, etc.`) explicitly acknowledges this vulnerability, confirming it is a known, unfixed issue.

**Remediation:**

Allowlist the specific fields that users are permitted to modify:

```typescript
app.put("/api/users/:id", async (req, res) => {
  // Allowlist only fields the user is permitted to update
  const { name, email, bio } = req.body;
  const allowedFields = { name, email, bio };

  // Remove undefined keys so unset fields aren't overwritten with NULL
  const filtered = Object.fromEntries(
    Object.entries(allowedFields).filter(([_, v]) => v !== undefined)
  );

  if (Object.keys(filtered).length === 0) {
    return res.status(400).json({ error: "No valid fields to update" });
  }

  await db.query("UPDATE users SET ? WHERE id = ?", [filtered, req.params.id]);
  res.json({ updated: true });
});

app.post("/api/register", async (req, res) => {
  const { name, email, password } = req.body;
  const user = { name, email, password, createdAt: new Date() };
  await db.query("INSERT INTO users SET ?", [user]);
  res.json({ id: user.id });
});
```

