### CYBER-002: Unspecified Endianness in Multi-Byte Reads

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Rule ID** | CYBER-002 |
| **Severity** | **Medium** |
| **CWE** | CWE-188 (Reliance on Data/Memory Layout) |
| **Confidence** | 85% |

**Description:**

Lines 7 and 9 cast raw byte pointers to `*const u32` and `*const u64` and dereference them directly. This performs a native-endian read, meaning the parsed values will differ depending on the target architecture (little-endian x86 vs. big-endian ARM/MIPS/PPC). For a network packet parser, this is a correctness and portability defect that can lead to wildly incorrect `payload_len` values on non-matching architectures — directly feeding into the out-of-bounds read described in CYBER-001.

Additionally, casting an unaligned pointer (`ptr.add(4)`) to `*const u32` is **undefined behavior** on architectures that require alignment (CWE-188). While x86 tolerates unaligned access, this is not guaranteed.

**Remediation:**

Use `u32::from_le_bytes` / `u32::from_be_bytes` (or `from_ne_bytes` if native endian is intentional) as shown in the CYBER-001 remediation above. These functions operate on byte slices and have no alignment requirements.

